## Known Limitations and Superseded Assumptions

- The original roadmap assumed a Python-only CLI; the product is now
  Groovy-default with Python fallback.
- The repository catalog intentionally contains fictional examples; verified
  organization-specific mappings belong in workspace overlays.
- Preflight primarily checks local readiness; reconciliation performs the
  implemented external observations.
- Diagnostic packs are declarative runners, not a completed migration of all
  historical ticket-local scripts.
- Windows is officially supported. Remaining Windows-specific test failures are
  tracked: 9 unit tests (path normalization, POSIX permissions, shell fixtures)
  and 15 parity tests (setup init actions, global config launcher, syntax-check
  output, and adapter PATH resolution).
- README requirements section still lists macOS or Linux only and needs update.
- `prompt.log` remains an append-only audit trail, not structured ticket state.
- Service discovery, catalog, and Jenkins use the shared command contract for
  parsing. The remaining operational command groups retain their imperative
  argument handling.
- Python remains runnable but does not yet consume `shared/command-contract.json`;
  JSON payload and exit-code parity is an acceptance gate for its later thaw.
- The supported dispatcher rejects the former top-level Jenkins path for both
  runtimes. Direct `ai-worklog-python jenkins` cleanup is deferred until Python
  adopts the recursive service contract.
- README command-reference generation and shell-completion generation from the
  contract are not implemented.
- Artifact downloading is implemented only in Groovy. The frozen Python runtime
  has no corresponding action or parity coverage.
- Bulk artifact downloading and caller-selected destination overrides remain
  out of scope.
- Jira comment posting, issue transitions, assignee changes, issue creation,
  attachment downloading, arbitrary JQL, and custom-field updates are deferred.
  Historical worklogs show recurring demand for closing comments and status
  transitions, while deployment custom fields remain owned by the separate
  Jenkins Jira integration.
- Live Jira enrichment in `ticket prepare`, Jira connectivity in preflight,
  summary snapshot persistence, and day-start orchestration are deferred.
- The retained `integrations/jira/jira-ticket-info.groovy` implementation is a
  temporary comparison reference and is no longer a supported launcher.
- Python does not implement the Jira service operator. Future parity work must
  reproduce command usage, JSON reports, exit codes, pagination, redaction,
  verification, and dry-run/apply behavior.
- Python does not implement the Automox service operator. Future parity work
  must reproduce all 19 actions, output formats, pagination, redaction, exit
  codes, and mutation gates.
- Automox SSH agent operations, batch enrollment, and `setup-agents.sh`
  wrappers remain intentionally outside the framework operator.
- Automox live verification does not execute POST, PUT, or DELETE requests;
  applied mutation paths remain covered by injected transport tests until an
  explicitly approved external test is performed.
- The standalone public-content check still flags pre-existing Jira test
  fixtures that contain an organization ticket prefix. Automox files pass the
  same identifier policy after technical SHA identifiers are allowlisted.
- Python does not implement the New Relic service operator. Future parity
  work must reproduce all 27 actions, profile resolution, JSON reports, exit
  codes, pagination, redaction, dry-run/apply gates, and Terraform export
  behavior.
- New Relic live verification is limited to read-only actions and mutation
  dry runs; production SaaS writes with `--apply` are not part of the public
  verification baseline.
- New Relic delete operations and host-side infra/license/restart mutations
  remain outside the framework operator boundary by design.
- Hardcoded credential removal in legacy reference scripts under
  `scripts/newrelic/` is tracked separately and must not be conflated with
  operator implementation.

## Continuation Procedure

Before resuming implementation:

1. Re-read this document and the current repository history.
2. Audit both repositories because statuses may have changed.
3. Run existing Python, Groovy, parity, and ai-vault validation suites.
4. Confirm the selected roadmap phase and its exact write boundary.
5. Enter the required governance mode before planning or editing.
6. Update this document after verified implementation work.
7. Keep credentials, private identifiers, and operational evidence outside the
   repositories.
8. Do not commit or push unless the user explicitly changes the standing rule.
